\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\min \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](images/34/3449b02c92b2b2534241d33f7b6288d8433eb907b5a97ad255aa9fd5022d5971.png)\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}=6{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/07/07bb866fbd14af52b5f3d01c91f6f58e1a7ef5bc4d70ada8c3a745035cae9e92.png)   \n\nSince $\\mathbf{v}$ is already normalized ($\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|=1$), we conclude that,   ![](images/46/467bac248fa9988f4ee41bb9adaaf5e7ecbdf0e7e47b66a091ff5edf799e6799.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198089 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12663" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198089 #edb/ref 1071 "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=9x_1^2-24x_1x_2+16x_2^2,$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1,$ is equal to $0.$ Find a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ at which the minimum is attained." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198089 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8a1adb2a-e9c5-52e1-bfa0-6977884fae42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198089 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\min \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](images/61/613dd6029260150b75d9f037c947fbd3a3ab05cd63b74cb3ab29fdd4f2a0963c.png)\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/dc/dc259d743780ce58cf437ef11518a22a8eb551af9a934d2ad29bc418cdb98db7.png)  \n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = 5,$ we get \n\n![](images/a0/a091f1b304062296158d63256cb19b2fe30b67340863b0f4355f9f0740d0bd71.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198090 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12664" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198090 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})=16x_1^2+16x_1x_2+x_2^2$ given that $16x_1^2 + x_2^2=16,$ and find the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ at which the minimum is obtained." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198090 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a6abb2f6-6e59-59ac-8549-62d25b3b6e9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198090 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n![](images/fa/fab48c5c9c4d9adee4f270c6138d0f69317cfa59ad9d6f5dab2d4cad15d368c6.png)\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x_1^2}{1^2}+\\dfrac{x_2^2}{4^2}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=1$ (along the $x_1$-axis) and $b=4$ (along the $x_2$-axis). \n\nIf we introduce new variables\n\n$\\qquad$ $y_1={x_1} \\quad$ and $\\quad y_2 = \\dfrac{x_2}{4},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into $y_1^2+y_2^2 = 1,$ or $\\|\\mathbf{y}\\|=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinates matrix \n![](images/30/3042cf8eadb699a70b70daced51411fa570ec9cdf3b7b270943fe790739a5364.png) \n\nwe obtain that $\\mathbf{x} = P \\mathbf{y}.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = (P\\mathbf{y})^T A \\, (P\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{y}^T \\! (P^TAP) \\mathbf{y}= Q'(\\mathbf{y}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since $P^T=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}16 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 8 \\\\ 32 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 32 \\\\ 32 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we minimise $Q'(\\mathbf{y})$ when $\\| \\mathbf{y} \\|=1,$ as usual. Calculating the eigenvalues $B,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}16-\\lambda  & 32 \\\\ 32 & 16-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (16-\\lambda )(16-\\lambda )-1\\,024 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-32\\lambda -768 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -48)(\\lambda +16) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =48,\\,-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\min \\{ Q'(\\mathbf{y}) \\: | \\: \\| \\mathbf{y} \\|=1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}} = -16.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}=-16{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-16I) & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 32 \\\\ 32 & 16\\end{aligned}]+16[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}32 & 32 \\\\ 32 & 32\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-(-16I))\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/df/df1ad3d9484608d37e762f74d2a61776c8f82f8eb650dcb0a37f47c5037678c4.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](images/c8/c869aa1eb73c078eaac66201b1062b2d55a45d90ec2010b6af532ca601498a53.png) \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{min}} & =P{u}_{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198442 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198442 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198442 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198442 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198442 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198442 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198442 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186378955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198442 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198442 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198442 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186389636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198442 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198442 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186442114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198442 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Are Attained on the Unit Sphere" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198442 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 